fix(mcp): address a linked worktree across the mount boundary (#215, #241)

describe_workspace refused a linked worktree. `git worktree list` does not
answer in one namespace: asked from the main tree it echoes the path it was
handed, and asked from a linked worktree it answers the path RECORDED when
that worktree was created, which is the host's. The tool compared the host's
spelling of the repository against the mount's spelling of the tree, got a
path made of `..` segments, and refused.

Measured against the deployment before the fix:

  /projects/<name>/.claude/worktrees/<id> is outside the repository at
  <host-checkout>/<name>

The refusal was correct about the paths and wrong about the world, and what
it refused was an agent's own worktree, which is the arrangement agents
actually run in.

Symlink resolution is not the answer, and was already tried: relativeWorktree
resolves both sides, which is what fixed the macOS /var vs /private/var case
it was written for. It cannot reach this one. The two roots are separate bind
mounts of one directory, so neither is a symlink of the other.

So the translation the tool ALREADY owns for `cwd` is applied to git's answer
too. A repository under neither root is refused by name rather than guessed:
a guessed repository is the wrong identifier in an append-only record.

Driven by MCP-071, MCP-072, MCP-073 and MCP-074, written first and observed
failing. MCP-072 reproduced the deployment's refusal verbatim.

